Keep the echo model at a call's start while the linear filter takes no echo away

At a call's start with music through speakers, AEC3 marks its linear filter usable well
before it cancels anything: the filter converges on a voice but not on sustained,
overlapping notes, and its full-band ERLE stays near 0 for seconds. The residual echo is
then taken from the linear filter's output, which still holds the whole echo, against an
estimate S2 the filter makes far too low, and the suppressor lets the music back.

Until the linear filter first takes echo away (its full-band ERLE log2 reaching 1, 3 dB;
once reached, for the echo controller's life, so realigns and restarts after it keep
stock AEC3), the residual echo estimator now also keeps the estimate of its non-linear
echo model, the render times the echo path gain, and takes the larger of the two. The
gain is bounded by what the microphone heard: every 12 blocks, over 1.15 s, margin times
the quietest 48 ms of the capture, after taking off four times its stationary noise,
against the render read at least 0.6 s earlier; the lowest such bound is kept. It applies
only once that render is as loud as active render, and only while the other side plays
without pauses (no 96 ms of the render under `pauses` times its average in the window),
so a voice, with its pauses, keeps stock AEC3. A change of delay or gain starts the
measurement over and keeps the bound and the noise.

Fogata-Aec3KeepEchoModelWhileErleIsLow/margin:<x>,pauses:<y>/: margin from 0 (left out,
stock AEC3) to 100, default 4 (6 dB); pauses from 0 to 1, default 0.5 (3 dB). A value
out of range keeps the default.

AEC3 alone at 16 kHz, 8 s from a call's start (the other side's music or speech-like
sound, through a room 10 dB under it at 60 ms or 20 dB under at 200 ms, over the room's
noise): the music let through from 1 to 6 s -17.9 dB before, -18.4 after (-19.9 with
pauses:0.1), at its loudest 5 s -1.1 before, -1.8 after (-3.4); with the echo 20 dB
under, -17.4 before, -17.8 after (-18.6). A voice, double talk from the call's start
through speakers, changes of output and a headset with the room's noise alone as before.
A headset with music and the person talking from the start: held down 2.27 s before,
2.54 s after (0.5 s more with pauses:0.1); double talk over a voice at the call's start
0.9 dB more held down with pauses:0.1. Keeping the echo model with no bound let 10 to 15
dB less music through on a real call and 3.5 dB less in this simulation, which so
understates what the model kept gives.

